# Invention No. 8 in F major, BWV 779 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: bright and playful, a strict canon at the octave for its
# first seven bars. The subject is a leaping F major arpeggio in eighths
# (after an eighth rest) and a bar of falling sixteenth turns; the bass
# follows the top one bar later, an octave lower. It wants lightness and
# an even, buoyant pulse rather than speed.
#
# Time signature: 3/4. The Mutopia score has 33 full bars and a final
# bar 34 of a single quarter (the closing chord). The piece starts with
# an eighth rest inside bar 1, and the MIDI keeps that rest (first note
# an eighth into bar 1), so bar numbers here match the score and no
# pickup is needed.
#
# Tempo: quarter = 96. About 100 quarters in all, so about 1:03.
# Track times (approximate): Glenn Gould (Sony, 1964) 1:05, about 92;
# Gustav Leonhardt (harpsichord, Apple Music reissue) 1:06, about 91;
# Andras Schiff (ECM, clavichord) 0:56, about 107; Angela Hewitt
# (Hyperion, her own discography page) 0:44, about 136, the fastest.
# 96 sits with Gould and Leonhardt, a touch quicker: unhurried, so the
# sixteenth turns sparkle without blurring.
#
# Structure (read from the Mutopia score and checked against the MIDI;
# the binary division at bar 12 and the key areas C major (8), D minor
# (22-23), B-flat (26) and F (29-30) follow Rachel Hocking's study guide;
# teoria.com notes that 19-26 only sounds like canon):
#   1-2    Subject, top, F major (the bass is silent in bar 1).
#   2-3    Subject, bottom, in canon at the octave, one bar behind.
#   4-7    The canon goes on: a three-bar broken-chord sequence, both
#          hands a bar apart, moving to C major.
#   8-11   Episode in C major: falling sixteenth turns against arpeggios,
#          cadence in C major at 11-12 (end of the first half).
#   12-13  Second half: subject in the bass, C major.
#   13-14  Subject, top, C major, now the top follows the bass.
#   15     Diminished-seventh exchange, turning to G minor.
#   16-17  Subject, top, G minor.
#   17-18  Subject, bottom, G minor.
#   19-23  Episode: imitation that only sounds like canon, a cycle of
#          fifths to D minor (22-23).
#   24-25  Falling sequence from D minor to B-flat.
#   26-29  Bars 4-7 return a fifth lower, starting in B-flat (the
#          subdominant) and leading home.
#   30-34  Bars 8-12 return in F major: final cadence at 33-34.

tempo 96

voice mid 0          # two voices only: there is no middle voice to set back

dyn 1 mf             # bars 1-11: the first half, F major to C major
lead 2-3 bottom      # the canon entry in the bass (bar 1 is the top alone)

dyn 12 mp            # bars 12-15: the second half starts with the
                     # subject again, now in C: an echo, a softer manual
lead 12-13 bottom    # subject, bass, C major
lead 13-14 top       # subject, top, C major (bar 13 shared: canon)

dyn 16 mf            # bars 16-25: G minor and the sequences to D minor
lead 16-17 top       # subject, top, G minor
lead 17-18 bottom    # subject, bass, G minor (bar 17 shared: canon)

dyn 26 f             # bars 26-34: the return of bars 4-11, home to F

rit 2 0.75           # only into the final cadence; bar 34 is a single
                     # quarter, so this starts early in bar 32
